depths, which comparison group — is a garden of forking paths [Gelman and Loken, 2014].

From correlation to cause. Nothing in this paper is causal. Establishing that a representation
participates in producing an output requires intervention: activation patching and its relatives [Meng
et al., 2022, Wang et al., 2022], with the metric and corruption choices that Zhang and Nanda [2024]
show materially change the conclusions. We specify such an experiment in Appendix C but did not
have the compute to run it.


3            The statistic and its nulls

Notation. Fix an example with a correct state c and a competitor state s. Let ℓ ∈ {0, . . . , L − 1}
index positions at which the residual stream is read, and let rℓ (·) be the rank of a token in the decoded
vocabulary distribution at position ℓ, with rank 1 the argmax and smaller meaning better. We write
layer only when quoting the claim as it is commonly stated, and depth for the positions actually
measured, since a model with L − 1 decoder layers is read at L residual-stream positions. Define
                           [︁                   ]︁             ∑︁ [︁                   ]︁
                   E = ⊮ ∃ℓ : rℓ (c) < rℓ (s) ,        G =         ℓ ⊮ rℓ (c) < rℓ (s) .

E is the existence statistic behind the claim type; G is its graded form.

E saturates and cannot be tested. Figure 1 makes both points without a model. Panel (a): for
two exchangeable series, P (E = 1) rises steeply in L and lies between 0.840 and 1.000 at L = 29
depending on the noise level; a series in which c is genuinely worse than s still yields E = 1 in 91.5%
of examples. Panel (b): under Null A below, with c given a genuine disadvantage, E is observed at
0.998 against a null mass at 0.999 with standard deviation 5.0 × 10−4 — no separation — whereas
G is observed at 11.86 against a null mean of 14.50 with standard deviation 0.138, roughly 19 null
standard deviations away. The same effect is invisible to one statistic and overwhelming to the other.
Panel (c): across true offsets from −1 to +1, E ranges only from 0.888 to 0.995 while G/L moves
monotonically from 0.318 to 0.685. In the case study E = 1 in both directions in every example, so
the null is an exact point mass and p = 1 by construction.

Null A: whole-example label swap. To test whether the identity of the correct state carries
information, swap c and s for an entire example across all L depths at once, and recompute the
statistic. The swap must be applied to the whole series. Swapping independently at each depth
destroys within-example cross-depth correlation and yields a falsely narrow null for a maximum
statistic — it would make almost any E look extreme.


                                                                                                        3
Null B: outcome shuffle with the maximum inside. To test whether a per-depth measure separates
two outcome groups, shuffle the group labels across examples and, within each permutation, recom-
pute the measure at every depth in the analysis band and take the maximum. Comparing the observed
maximum against this distribution is a max-T procedure [Westfall and Young, 1993, Nichols and
Holmes, 2002] and is what prices in having chosen the best depth. Comparing a post-hoc-selected
depth against a per-depth null instead is the error this control exists to prevent.

Split-half depth selection. Passing Null B shows that some depth separates the groups; it does not
show that a particular depth does. We select the best depth on one half of the data and evaluate it on
the held-out half, repeatedly, and report both the held-out effect and how often each depth is selected.
A measure whose selected depth is not reproducible across splits does not support a claim naming
that depth.

Excluded bands. Two ranges must be excluded before any “internal” claim. Concretely, for the 29
residual-stream positions of the case study we take the internal band to be depths 2–23, excluding
depths 0–1 as near-embedding and depths 24–28 as output-committed. Every later reference to
“the internal band” means exactly this range. Positions adjacent to the embedding carry little of the
model’s computation. Positions adjacent to the output are worse: at the final residual position the
lens is one norm and one unembedding away from the logits, so it is the model’s decision, and any
statistic separating outcomes there is circular [Kriegeskorte et al., 2009]. In the case study a group
separation measured at the last position is exactly 1.000 by construction.

4   Case study: stale-state tracking
Task and data. Each record places a named entity in a room, narrates a sequence of movements in
which that entity is relocated exactly once, and then asks which room it is in now. The relocation
makes the earlier stated room stale; answering correctly requires using the later mention rather than
the earlier one, and a failure is the model emitting the stale room. Records vary in complexity level,
in whether the tracked entity is an agent or an object, and in the number of distractor mentions (0, 3
or 4) and their position relative to the update. The dataset is 5,000 such records.

Model and configuration. Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct in bfloat16, eager attention (required to capture
attention probabilities), no quantization, chat prompt format, with an entity-aware prefill ending
exactly where the room name must appear; the readout is the single token at that position. All three
runs used transformers 5.14.1. Per-run configurations are committed, and record 5,112 s of GPU
wall-clock in total (1.42 h): 3.52 and 3.49 s per record for the two 29-depth sweeps and 0.577 s per
record for the three-depth sweep. The GPU model is not recorded by the probe, so we do not state
one.

Depth indexing. The model has 28 decoder layers. A pre-hook on the input of each layer plus a
post-hook on the output of the last gives 29 residual-stream positions, depths 0..28; depth 28 is the
final residual stream, immediately before the final norm and unembedding. Attention is indexed by
decoder layer, 0..27, so no attention exists at depth 28 — a structural fact, not a capture failure. We
say “depths” rather than “layers” throughout. Ranks are 1-based and the vocabulary is V = 152,064.

Runs. Three runs, all from the same 5,000-record dataset (Table 1). Behaviourally the model
answers correctly on 97.1% of records, emits the stale state on 2.7% (137 records), and produces an
unparseable answer on 0.2%. Failures are entirely concentrated: the stale-output rate is 0.00% at
complexity levels 1 and 2 and 8.22% at level 3, and 0.00% for object entities against 5.47% for agents.
This concentration is what makes the matched comparison in Section 5 necessary rather than optional.
The 137-failure run is the complete failure population, re-swept at all depths. Reconstructing this
required signature matching, because that export renumbered example identifiers (Appendix A); the
twelve failures common to both full-depth runs agree exactly on every rank column, so the two pool.

Measures. Logit lens: unembed(norm(hℓ )). J-lens: a first-order expansion of the model’s own
remaining computation, fℓ (0) + Jhℓ , where fℓ runs decoder layers ℓ..28 with the real frozen KV
cache. Nine attention columns record mass at the readout position. For each measure we use the
margin between c and s.


