5     Results
5.1   The claim fails four controls

Table 2 summarizes. The existence test fires in both directions in every failure, and in every success.
Under Null A it is invariant: because both directions already hold in all 137 examples, relabelling
cannot move it, so the null is an exact point mass and p = 1.00. This is the difference between a
statistic that is non-significant and one that is untestable; E is the latter.
The graded statistic G is testable, and it points the other way. The correct state wins 10.93 of 29
depths on average, against a label-swap null of 14.48 (95% interval [13.12, 15.82]), p < 10−4 . Under
J-lens, 8.41 against 14.34. The correct state wins fewer depths than chance under label exchange
— a finding in its own right, and the opposite of what the original claim asserted.

5.2   Two reported figures for one quantity

We had separately reported a mean best rank for the correct state of 47,343 and of 2.3. These are the
same lens on the same 137 examples. The only difference is the depth set over which the minimum
is taken: {5, 8, 13} gives 47,343.5; all 29 depths give 2.3. Restricting the full sweep to {5, 8, 13}
reproduces 47,343.5 exactly.
The favourable figure is an artifact of admitting output-committed depths, and inspecting where the
minimum falls shows why it cannot support the claim. The best rank occurs at depth ≥ 26 in 100%
of failures, and at that depth the correct state outranks the stale state in only 13.1% of cases: the
median stale rank there is 1 and the median correct rank is 2. The correct state is the runner-up at the
output, which is what a stale failure is. Where the correct state genuinely wins by the largest margin
(median depth 9), both tokens are buried — median ranks 24,183 against 103,440 out of 152,064.
The two regimes are disjoint, and the original claim was assembled by taking the existence test from
one and the impressive rank from the other.

5.3   The comparison groups are confounded

Every failure is a complexity-3, agent-entity, middle-distractor record; 70.7% of successes contain no
distractors at all, and only 29.3% are complexity-3. Any measure tracking task difficulty separates
these groups for reasons unrelated to the outcome. We therefore define a matched stratum (complexity
3, agent, middle distractor, 3–4 distractors), which retains 72 of 488 successes at full depth and 682 of
4,853 in the three-depth run; all 137 failures already lie inside it. Figure 2 shows the cost of ignoring
this: at depth 13 the J-lens AUC falls from 0.952 to 0.700 under matching.
The symmetry control and both Null A tests are within-failure analyses and are untouched by the
confound; the discrimination control’s conclusion also stands, since a statistic at 100% in both arms


