Figure 3 shows what the two groups look like across the whole stack. All four curves sit together
around rank 105 from the embedding to about depth 22, and separate only over the last five or six
positions. Averaged over the internal band the mean gap (stale rank minus correct rank, so positive
favours the correct token) is −16,569 in failures against +9,242 in matched successes: through most
of the stack of a failure the stale token is better ranked than the correct one, which is the opposite of
what the retracted claim asserted. Depth 22 is where the ordering starts to move, which is also where
the surviving attention signal sits.

5.4                                             What survives

Table 3 gives the matched, selection-corrected picture. Attention mass differential at depth 22
separates the groups with matched AUC 0.973 and held-out 0.970, selecting depth 22 in 90% of
splits; failures place more mass on the stale anchor and less on the update mention. It is the one
measure that survives every control.
Logit-lens margin does not survive. It clears Null B, but the depth it selects is reproducible in only
32% of splits, spreading across depths 12, 16, 18 and 23. The effect is real but diffuse, and no claim
naming a specific logit-lens depth is supported. This is precisely the distinction the split-half control
exists to draw, and it is invisible to a permutation test alone.

Per-head corroboration. The depth-22 result above is computed from per-example attention
aggregates. A separate per-head analysis ranks all 784 (depth, head) pairs by a difference in


