differences: for each head, let ∆ = (mass on the stale anchor) − (mass on the update mention), and
score the head by ∆fail − ∆succ . Of its top 20 heads, 15 fall in depths 19–22 and all 20 in depths
19–26, with 9 at depth 22 alone; the highest-scoring single head is at depth 23.
Two things about this statistic matter for how much weight it carries. It is computed against the
matched success set — the 72 records with a single stale and a single correct mention, which is the
regime all 137 failures occupy by construction and is what makes the broad per-head spans coincide
with the anchor and update spans — so it does not share the confound of Section 5. But it is the same
72 controls used for the depth-22 AUC. It is therefore corroboration by a different statistic computed
from different data on disk (per-head tensors rather than per-example aggregates), not corroboration
from an independent control group; the 72-record ceiling in Section 6 applies to both. It also supplies
the patching target in Appendix C.
The full attention sweep (Figure 4) locates discrimination at depths 19–25 and near chance through
the early and middle stack. Within the matched stratum two column pairs are identical, because
matched records carry a single stale mention, so the sweep covers seven distinct measures rather than
nine.

6         Limitations
Why the J-lens result is exploratory. J-lens is a first-order Taylor expansion, about the baseline
b = 0, of the model’s own remaining computation: jlensℓ = fℓ (0) + Jhℓ , with fℓ running decoder
layers ℓ . . . 28. A lens of that definition is pinned at one end. As ℓ → 28 there is less and less
computation left to expand — at the final depth f28 is just the norm and the unembedding — so a
faithful first-order readout must approach the model’s own output distribution. It does not. Figure 5
shows the divergence, and it is stark in the one place the answer is known: in a stale-output failure the
token the model emits is by construction its own rank-1 token, and the logit lens, being the model’s
norm and unembedding, ranks it 1 in 100% of failures at depth 28. J-lens places that same token at
median rank 74,329 at depth 26 and 61,842 at depth 27, deep in the tail of a 152,064-token vocabulary.
At depth 28 itself the expansion does not merely err, it vanishes: with a bias-free RMSNorm and a
bias-free unembedding, f28 (0) = 0 and f28 is positively homogeneous of degree zero, so J(h)h = 0


                                                                                               7
by Euler’s theorem and jlens28 ≡ 0. The resulting logits are uniform to three significant figures, and
the apparent agreement there is a tie artifact (Appendix D). An expansion that does not approach
the function it expands, at the end of the stack where there is almost nothing left to expand, is not
behaving as a readout of the remaining network. We therefore read the depth-5 J-lens margin as
a fixed, untrained linear functional J(0) h5 of the residual state that separates the matched groups
(AUC 0.888, held-out 0.882) — a real and reproducible separation, but not evidence about what
the remaining network would do with h5 . It is reported as exploratory, and the surviving attention
result does not depend on it. Auditing the probe’s self-check for this paper also surfaced two defects,
now fixed: a non-finite comparison passed silently because nan > 0.5 is false in IEEE 754, and the
docstring named a tolerance the code did not use.

Statistical power. The matched full-depth comparison rests on 72 successes. The three-depth run
supplies 682 matched successes but only at depths 5, 8 and 13. The failure population is capped at
137 — that is every failure this configuration produces, so a larger failure sample requires a harder
task, not more sampling.

Everything here is correlational. A measure that predicts an outcome need not participate in
producing it. We specify a patching experiment with its controls in Appendix C, including a control
for the failure mode in which a patch at the decision token simply transfers a donor’s answer — which
is answer-copying, not localization — but we did not have the compute to run it. No claim in this
paper is causal.

Scope. One model, one synthetic dataset, one prompt template. The concentration of all failures in
a single condition cell limits external validity independently of the statistical issues.


7   Recommendations

1. Do not report an existence-over-depth statistic as evidence. If both directions hold in every
   example it is untestable, not merely weak. Report the graded form, which has a null.
2. State the depth set with every depth-selected statistic. Two of our own figures for one quantity
   differed by four orders of magnitude through depth-set choice alone, and the discrepancy is
   unrecoverable from the numbers.
3. Exclude output-committed positions from “internal” claims. At the last residual position the
   lens is the decision, and the statistic is circular [Kriegeskorte et al., 2009].
4. Match the comparison groups, and report raw beside matched so the confound size stays
   visible.
5. Correct for depth selection twice: a max-T permutation for whether any depth separates the
   groups, and split-half selection for whether a named depth reproducibly does. The second caught
   a result the first passed.
The effect of these is to raise the evidentiary bar for a common class of interpretability claim, including
one of our own. We think the trade is favourable: of the results we previously reported, the two that
survive are better localized and better supported than the one we lost.

Broader impact. This is a methodological correction, and its intended effect is to make a common
class of internal-representation claim harder to assert without controls. The foreseeable downside is
the mirror image: if the controls are applied only in their strictest form, genuine internal-representation
effects become harder to publish, which is why we recommend the graded, matched and split-half
statistics as replacements rather than proposing that such claims be abandoned. We foresee no other
societal impact from this work.


