Figure 5: As Figure 3, under J-lens. Note the behaviour at depths 26–27, where almost no computation
remains to expand: a faithful first-order readout would be converging on the model’s output there,
and this one is not. See Section 6 and Appendix D.

For contrast only, the same internal-band mean gaps computed on the unmatched groups (137
failures against all 488 successes) are −16,569 for failures and +5,612 for successes under the logit
lens. The failure figure is unchanged because all 137 failures already lie inside the matched stratum;
the success figure is not, which is the confound in one number. These unmatched values are reported
here and nowhere else.

C                                              Planned patching experiment
For each of the 137 failures, patch attention-head outputs at depths 19–22 from a condition-matched
success in the same cell, and measure the rate at which the output flips to the correct room. The
target heads are the top-ranked ones from the per-head analysis in Section 5, which concentrate in
depths 19–22. That ranking lives in reports/head ranking.csv, a committed artifact produced by
analyze heads.py with its default --baseline matched, which is why the file records nsucc = 72. It
is derived from the per-(depth, head) attention tensors, which are not included in the supplementary
archive because of their size, but the ranking itself and the script that produces it both are. Controls:
random heads at the same depths; heads ranked lowest by the same committed head ranking; and
mean-ablation of the top-k stale-preferring heads. A patch applied at the decision token can transfer
the donor’s answer outright, which is answer-copying rather than localization; the donor-mismatch
control detects this. Metrics and corruption choices follow Zhang and Nanda [2024]. Script:
patch probe.py. Not run: no GPU was available.


D                                              The J-lens linearization gap
Section 6 demotes the J-lens result because the lens fails a convergence check that follows from its
own definition. This appendix states what would actually diagnose it.

Depth 28 and depths 26–27 are separable failures. At the final depth the remaining computation is
f28 (h) = lm head(RMSNorm(h)), and in this architecture both parts are bias-free: Qwen2RMSNorm


                                                                                                                          10
carries a weight and no bias, and lm head is constructed as nn.Linear(hidden, vocab, bias=False)
(verified in the transformers 5.14.1 source, the version all three runs used). Two consequences
follow, and they are not what one might hope.
First, f28 (0) = 0: with no bias anywhere, the expansion’s anchor term vanishes. Second, RMSNorm
divides out the scale of its input, so f28 is positively homogeneous of degree zero, f28 (αh) = f28 (h)
for α > 0; by Euler’s theorem the directional derivative along h of a degree-zero homogeneous
function is zero, so J(h) h = 0. Therefore jlens28 = f28 (0) + Jh28 = 0 identically. At the one
depth where the expansion ought to be trivially exact, it instead annihilates the signal.
The data carry that signature exactly. At depth 28 the J-lens logits have mean absolute value 2.2×10−6
— six orders of magnitude below their size at depths 26–27 — and mean pcorrect = 6.58 × 10−6 ,
which is the uniform value 1/152,064 to three significant figures. The correct–stale margin is exactly
zero in 28.5% of failures and both tokens tie at rank 1 in the same 28.5%, which no real distribution
produces but an all-zero logit vector does under a rank convention that counts strictly-greater scores.
The 71.5% “rank 1” at depth 28 is thus a tie artifact and bounds nothing; in particular it is not a
measurement of the bf16 JVP path’s numerical precision.
Depths 26–27 are a different failure. There the logits are O(1) and no margin is exactly zero, so the
expansion produces a genuine distribution — it is simply the wrong one, placing the model’s own
argmax token at median rank 74,329 and 61,842. That is linearization error, not annihilation. The
two should not be conflated: a fix for one need not touch the other.

The right diagnostic is the linearization error, not the replay. jlensℓ = fℓ (0)+Jhℓ approximates
fℓ (hℓ ) by a first-order expansion about b = 0. The quantity that governs whether it is a readout of
the remaining network is therefore the per-depth discrepancy between fℓ (hℓ ) — the true output of
running layers ℓ . . . 28 on the real activation — and jlensℓ . Measuring it for every ℓ, as a KL between
the two softmaxes rather than a max logit difference, would say directly how far the expansion is
from the function, and where. We have not run that measurement; it needs a GPU.
Figure 5 answers the question qualitatively and, for the purposes of this paper, sufficiently. Because
b = 0 is far from the real activation, the expansion is being evaluated well outside any radius in
which a first-order term dominates, and the residual fℓ (hℓ ) − jlensℓ is large enough at ℓ = 26, 27 to
move the model’s own argmax token into the tail of the vocabulary. A baseline nearer the data — the
previous depth’s activation, which the probe already supports — would be the first thing to try.

What the dtype ladder would and would not settle. The probe ships a replay self-check, and a
diagnostic (jlens diag.py) that runs it across dtypes. Neither bears on the above. The self-check
validates that the truncated stack reproduces the model, that is, that fℓ is computed correctly; the
dtype ladder asks whether any discrepancy there is numerical precision. Both concern the replay. The
convergence failure documented here is a property of the expansion, and would survive an exactly
faithful fℓ computed in double precision. We therefore do not treat running the ladder as open work
for this result. For completeness: the self-check was not enabled for any of the three runs (all three
configurations record self check: false), and a separate diagnostic invocation on bfloat16 that
exceeded the check’s threshold was not committed, so no number from it is cited.

E    Reproduction
Every number in this paper is produced by a committed script over committed CSVs. No GPU is
required.
         python3 controls check.py
         python3 controls check2.py
         python3 sim/existence null sim.py
         python3 depth profile matched.py
         python3 paper extras.py
         python3 run metadata.py
         cd paper/final && ./build.sh

build.sh is the only supported way to build the paper: it compiles both the anonymized and named
PDFs from the single source, diffs their text outside a documented whitelist, and asserts the venue


                                                   11
notice. Outputs land in reports/. Seeds are fixed (5,000 permutations, 500 splits). The paper pulls
its figures from reports/ at build time.

F   LLM usage
The analysis, controls and this manuscript were developed with the assistance of a large language
model acting as a coding and analysis assistant. All statistical procedures were specified by the
authors; all code and results are reproducible from the committed scripts, and every number in the
paper is traceable to a script output file. No model or vendor names are given in this build.




